CONTEXT FormalAmpersand IN ENGLISH
-- This file specifies systems of several contexts. It is the formal basis for multi-context Ampersand.
-- Its mathematical counterpart is the article "Multi-Context Information Systems in Ampersand" (2026).
-- The two are kept consistent: every RULE below corresponds to a requirement or a theorem in the article.
--
-- The compiler implements this specification (Ampersand.Input.Qualify and Ampersand.FSpec.ToFSpec.ADL2Plug).
-- FormalAmpersand.adl does not include this file yet, so the compiler embeds it without using it.
-- A comment that starts with OPEN marks a choice that awaits a decision by Stef Joosten.
--
-- This file can be checked on its own. For that purpose it repeats the declarations it borrows
-- from Concepts.adl, Contexts.adl, Relations.adl, Rules.adl and Terms.adl, in the same wording.

PURPOSE CONTEXT FormalAmpersand
{+A system of several contexts consists of contexts that include one another.
Every context has declarations of its own, a database of its own, and a backend of its own.
This file specifies which declarations hold in which context,
how a script refers to a declaration of another context,
and which context stores, reads and writes which population.
+}

PURPOSE PATTERN FormalAmpersand.ContextsAndDatabases
{+A context is the unit of deployment.
The compiler generates one database and one backend with one API from one context,
so that a context can be deployed by itself.

The name of a context identifies the context.
A script states it after the keyword `CONTEXT`, and everything between `CONTEXT` and `ENDCONTEXT` belongs to the context with that name.
A context can be written in several fragments, in one file or in several files;
fragments with the same context name are united, and the statement `INCLUDE "file"` inside a fragment brings the text of another file in.
So a context is not a file.

A context changes over time, and each stage has a version.
The existing and the desired system in a migration are two versions of one context name, and they are two contexts, each with a database of its own.
So a context is determined by its name and its version together.
+}
PATTERN FormalAmpersand.ContextsAndDatabases
    CONCEPT FormalAmpersand.Context "A context is a set of declarations that is deployed as one information system, with one database and one backend."
    CONCEPT FormalAmpersand.Database "A database is the store that holds the population of one context."
    CONCEPT FormalAmpersand.Version "A version is the designation that the production process gives to one stage of a context."
    CLASSIFY FormalAmpersand.ContextName ISA FormalAmpersand.Name
    REPRESENT FormalAmpersand.Name TYPE ALPHANUMERIC

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.name[FormalAmpersand.Context*FormalAmpersand.ContextName] [UNI,TOT]
    MEANING "The name of a context."
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.version[FormalAmpersand.Context*FormalAmpersand.Version] [UNI,TOT]
    MEANING "The version of a context."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.ContextIdentity LABEL "identity of a context" :
        FormalAmpersand.name[FormalAmpersand.Context*FormalAmpersand.ContextName];FormalAmpersand.name[FormalAmpersand.Context*FormalAmpersand.ContextName]~
        /\ FormalAmpersand.version;FormalAmpersand.version~ |- I[FormalAmpersand.Context]
    MEANING "Two contexts with the same name and the same version are one context."

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.database[FormalAmpersand.Context*FormalAmpersand.Database] [UNI,TOT,INJ,SUR]
    MEANING "Every context has one database of its own, and every database realises one context. No population is stored twice."
    -- The name of a database is a fact of the deployment. By default it consists of the name of the context and its version.
ENDPATTERN

PURPOSE PATTERN FormalAmpersand.Inclusion
{+A context uses the declarations and the population of another context by including it.
The statement `CONTEXT A INCLUDES B` says so. It stands outside every `CONTEXT` block, because it relates contexts.
It can tell in which file B is to be found, and it can give B another name for use in A:
`CONTEXT Migration INCLUDES Kurk FROM "kurk_desired.adl" AS new`.
An inclusion has no effect on the declarations of B.

In the script of A, a name of B carries a prefix, which is the name of B or the alias of the inclusion.
Both remain usable.
If A includes two contexts with the same name, the name does not tell them apart, and each of the two inclusions needs an alias.
In general, every inclusion needs a prefix that fits no other context that A includes.

To compile A, the compiler needs the declarations of every context that A includes directly or indirectly, and of no other context.
A context must be deployable without the contexts that include it.
That is why inclusion is acyclic: in a cycle, no context could be deployed before the others.
The reflexive and transitive closure of an acyclic relation is a partial order.
+}
PATTERN FormalAmpersand.Inclusion
    CONCEPT FormalAmpersand.Inclusion "An inclusion is the statement that one context includes another context."
    CONCEPT FormalAmpersand.Alias "An alias is a name that an including context gives to an included context."
    CLASSIFY FormalAmpersand.Alias ISA FormalAmpersand.Name

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.includer[FormalAmpersand.Inclusion*FormalAmpersand.Context] [UNI,TOT]
    MEANING "The includer of an inclusion is the context that is named before the keyword INCLUDES."
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.included[FormalAmpersand.Inclusion*FormalAmpersand.Context] [UNI,TOT]
    MEANING "The included context of an inclusion is the context that is named after the keyword INCLUDES."
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.alias[FormalAmpersand.Inclusion*FormalAmpersand.Alias] [UNI]
    MEANING "The alias of an inclusion is the name after the keyword AS, if the statement has one."

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.prefix[FormalAmpersand.Inclusion*FormalAmpersand.Name]
    MEANING "A prefix of an inclusion is a name by which the script of the includer can call the included context."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.PrefixDefinition LABEL "definition of prefix" :
        FormalAmpersand.prefix[FormalAmpersand.Inclusion*FormalAmpersand.Name]
        = FormalAmpersand.alias \/ FormalAmpersand.included;FormalAmpersand.name[FormalAmpersand.Context*FormalAmpersand.ContextName]
    MEANING "The prefixes of an inclusion are its alias and the name of the included context."

    RULE FormalAmpersand.EveryInclusionCanBeNamed LABEL "every inclusion can be named" :
        I[FormalAmpersand.Inclusion]
        |- (FormalAmpersand.prefix[FormalAmpersand.Inclusion*FormalAmpersand.Name]
            - (FormalAmpersand.includer;FormalAmpersand.includer~ - FormalAmpersand.included;FormalAmpersand.included~);FormalAmpersand.prefix[FormalAmpersand.Inclusion*FormalAmpersand.Name]
           );FormalAmpersand.prefix[FormalAmpersand.Inclusion*FormalAmpersand.Name]~
    MEANING "Every inclusion has a prefix that fits no other context that the same includer includes."
    -- So if a context includes two contexts with the same name, each of the two inclusions needs an alias.

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.includes[FormalAmpersand.Context*FormalAmpersand.Context]
    MEANING "Context A includes context B if an inclusion states so."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.IncludesDefinition LABEL "definition of includes" :
        FormalAmpersand.includes = FormalAmpersand.includer~;FormalAmpersand.included
    MEANING "The relation `includes` contains the pairs of includer and included context of all inclusions."

    RULE FormalAmpersand.InclusionIsAcyclic LABEL "inclusion is acyclic" :
        FormalAmpersand.includes+ |- -I[FormalAmpersand.Context]
    MEANING "No context includes itself, directly or indirectly."
    -- Consequence (article, lemma on the order of inclusion): includes+ is a strict partial order and includes* is a partial order.
    -- The relation `includes` itself is neither reflexive nor transitive.
ENDPATTERN

PURPOSE PATTERN FormalAmpersand.OwnershipAndVisibility
{+Every concept, relation and rule is declared in one context, which we call its owner.
The owner is part of the identity of a declaration:
the concept `Person` of a register and the concept `Person` of a permit system are two concepts.
A declaration is visible in its owner and in every context that includes its owner, directly or indirectly.

So the declarations that are visible in context A form the ordinary union of the declarations of the contexts that A reaches.
That union happens to be disjoint, because declarations with different owners differ.
It is not a disjoint union per inclusion.
If A includes B and C, and both include D, then D is one context with one database,
and the concept `Town` of D that A reaches through B is the same concept as the one it reaches through C.
+}
PATTERN FormalAmpersand.OwnershipAndVisibility
    CONCEPT FormalAmpersand.Declaration "A declaration is a concept, a relation or a rule that a context declares."
    CONCEPT FormalAmpersand.Concept ""
    CONCEPT FormalAmpersand.Relation "A relation is a set of pairs."
    CONCEPT FormalAmpersand.Rule "A rule is a statement that must be satisfied in each context in which it is valid."
    CLASSIFY FormalAmpersand.Concept, FormalAmpersand.Relation, FormalAmpersand.Rule ISA FormalAmpersand.Declaration
    CLASSIFY FormalAmpersand.ConceptName, FormalAmpersand.RelationName, FormalAmpersand.RuleName ISA FormalAmpersand.Name

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.owner[FormalAmpersand.Declaration*FormalAmpersand.Context] [UNI,TOT]
    MEANING "The owner of a declaration is the context in which it is declared."
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.localName[FormalAmpersand.Declaration*FormalAmpersand.Name] [UNI,TOT]
    MEANING "The local name of a declaration is the name by which the script of its owner calls it. It contains no prefix."

    -- The existing relations of FormalAmpersand that register the declaring context are the three parts of `owner`.
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.context[FormalAmpersand.Concept*FormalAmpersand.Context] [UNI]
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.relsDefdIn[FormalAmpersand.Relation*FormalAmpersand.Context] [UNI]
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.context[FormalAmpersand.Rule*FormalAmpersand.Context] [UNI]
    RULE FormalAmpersand.OwnerOfConcept LABEL "owner of a concept" :
        FormalAmpersand.context[FormalAmpersand.Concept*FormalAmpersand.Context] = I[FormalAmpersand.Concept];FormalAmpersand.owner
    MEANING "The context of a concept is its owner."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.OwnerOfRelation LABEL "owner of a relation" :
        FormalAmpersand.relsDefdIn[FormalAmpersand.Relation*FormalAmpersand.Context] = I[FormalAmpersand.Relation];FormalAmpersand.owner
    MEANING "The context in which a relation is defined is its owner."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.OwnerOfRule LABEL "owner of a rule" :
        FormalAmpersand.context[FormalAmpersand.Rule*FormalAmpersand.Context] = I[FormalAmpersand.Rule];FormalAmpersand.owner
    MEANING "The context of a rule is its owner."

    -- Likewise for the three existing relations called `name`.
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.name[FormalAmpersand.Concept*FormalAmpersand.ConceptName] [UNI,TOT]
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.name[FormalAmpersand.Relation*FormalAmpersand.RelationName] [UNI,TOT]
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.name[FormalAmpersand.Rule*FormalAmpersand.RuleName] [UNI]
    RULE FormalAmpersand.LocalNameOfConcept LABEL "local name of a concept" :
        FormalAmpersand.name[FormalAmpersand.Concept*FormalAmpersand.ConceptName] = I[FormalAmpersand.Concept];FormalAmpersand.localName;I[FormalAmpersand.ConceptName]
    MEANING "The name of a concept is its local name."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.LocalNameOfRelation LABEL "local name of a relation" :
        FormalAmpersand.name[FormalAmpersand.Relation*FormalAmpersand.RelationName] = I[FormalAmpersand.Relation];FormalAmpersand.localName;I[FormalAmpersand.RelationName]
    MEANING "The name of a relation is its local name."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.LocalNameOfRule LABEL "local name of a rule" :
        FormalAmpersand.name[FormalAmpersand.Rule*FormalAmpersand.RuleName] = I[FormalAmpersand.Rule];FormalAmpersand.localName;I[FormalAmpersand.RuleName]
    MEANING "The name of a rule is its local name."

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.source[FormalAmpersand.Relation*FormalAmpersand.Concept] [UNI,TOT]
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.target[FormalAmpersand.Relation*FormalAmpersand.Concept] [UNI,TOT]

    -- Identity. A declaration is determined by its owner, its local name and its kind.
    -- Relations.adl contains the rule `eqRelation`, which identifies a relation by name and signature alone.
    -- That rule holds within one context only; with several contexts, RelationIdentity replaces it.
    RULE FormalAmpersand.ConceptIdentity LABEL "identity of a concept" :
        I[FormalAmpersand.Concept];(FormalAmpersand.owner;FormalAmpersand.owner~ /\ FormalAmpersand.localName;FormalAmpersand.localName~);I[FormalAmpersand.Concept] |- I[FormalAmpersand.Concept]
    MEANING "Two concepts with the same owner and the same local name are one concept."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.RuleIdentity LABEL "identity of a rule" :
        I[FormalAmpersand.Rule];(FormalAmpersand.owner;FormalAmpersand.owner~ /\ FormalAmpersand.localName;FormalAmpersand.localName~);I[FormalAmpersand.Rule] |- I[FormalAmpersand.Rule]
    MEANING "Two rules with the same owner and the same local name are one rule."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.RelationIdentity LABEL "identity of a relation" :
        I[FormalAmpersand.Relation];(FormalAmpersand.owner;FormalAmpersand.owner~ /\ FormalAmpersand.localName;FormalAmpersand.localName~);I[FormalAmpersand.Relation]
        /\ FormalAmpersand.source;FormalAmpersand.source~ /\ FormalAmpersand.target;FormalAmpersand.target~ |- I[FormalAmpersand.Relation]
    MEANING "Two relations with the same owner, the same local name, the same source and the same target are one relation."

    -- Visibility.
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.visible[FormalAmpersand.Declaration*FormalAmpersand.Context]
    MEANING "A declaration is visible in a context if that context is its owner or includes its owner, directly or indirectly."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.VisibleDefinition LABEL "definition of visible" :
        FormalAmpersand.visible = FormalAmpersand.owner;FormalAmpersand.includes*~
    MEANING "The declarations that are visible in a context are the declarations of the contexts it reaches by inclusion."

    -- The existing relations called `valid` are the three parts of `visible`.
    -- Contexts.adl computes them from the patterns of one context (rules AllValidConcepts and AllValidRules);
    -- those two rules have to admit the declarations of included contexts when this file is taken into use.
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.valid[FormalAmpersand.Concept*FormalAmpersand.Context]
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.valid[FormalAmpersand.Relation*FormalAmpersand.Context]
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.valid[FormalAmpersand.Rule*FormalAmpersand.Context]
    RULE FormalAmpersand.ValidConceptIsVisible LABEL "valid concepts" :
        FormalAmpersand.valid[FormalAmpersand.Concept*FormalAmpersand.Context] = I[FormalAmpersand.Concept];FormalAmpersand.visible
    MEANING "A concept is valid in the contexts in which it is visible."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.ValidRelationIsVisible LABEL "valid relations" :
        FormalAmpersand.valid[FormalAmpersand.Relation*FormalAmpersand.Context] = I[FormalAmpersand.Relation];FormalAmpersand.visible
    MEANING "A relation is valid in the contexts in which it is visible."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.ValidRuleIsVisible LABEL "valid rules" :
        FormalAmpersand.valid[FormalAmpersand.Rule*FormalAmpersand.Context] = I[FormalAmpersand.Rule];FormalAmpersand.visible
    MEANING "A rule is valid in the contexts in which it is visible."

    -- A declaration mentions only what its owner can see.
    RULE FormalAmpersand.SignatureIsNear LABEL "signature is near" :
        (FormalAmpersand.source \/ FormalAmpersand.target)~;I[FormalAmpersand.Relation];FormalAmpersand.owner
        |- I[FormalAmpersand.Concept];FormalAmpersand.owner;(I[FormalAmpersand.Context] \/ FormalAmpersand.includes~)
    MEANING "The source and the target of a relation belong to the owner of that relation or to a context that this owner includes directly."

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.formalTerm[FormalAmpersand.Rule*FormalAmpersand.Term] [UNI]
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.usedIn[FormalAmpersand.Relation*FormalAmpersand.Term]
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.dependsOn[FormalAmpersand.Rule*FormalAmpersand.Declaration]
    MEANING "A rule depends on a concept or a relation if a change in the population of that concept or relation can change the violations of the rule."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.RuleDependsOnItsRelations LABEL "rule depends on its relations" :
        FormalAmpersand.formalTerm;FormalAmpersand.usedIn~ |- FormalAmpersand.dependsOn
    MEANING "A rule depends on every relation that occurs in its term."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.RuleIsLocalToItsReach LABEL "rule is local to its reach" :
        FormalAmpersand.dependsOn |- I[FormalAmpersand.Rule];FormalAmpersand.owner;FormalAmpersand.visible~
    MEANING "A rule depends only on concepts and relations that are visible in its owner."
ENDPATTERN

PURPOSE PATTERN FormalAmpersand.Names
{+A declaration has one local name, which its owner gave it.
Inside its own context, a script refers to it by that name, as it always did.
From another context, a script puts a prefix in front: `Kurk.r`, or `new.r` if the inclusion has the alias `new`.
So a reference consists of an optional prefix and a local name, and what it refers to depends on the context in which it is written.

Two requirements make this mechanism sound.
Unambiguous: a reference denotes one context, and in that context at most one declaration of each kind.
Complete: every declaration of a context that A includes can be referred to in A.
Unambiguous does not mean that a declaration has one reference:
with an alias, `Kurk.r` and `new.r` can be two references to one relation.
+}
PATTERN FormalAmpersand.Names
    CONCEPT FormalAmpersand.Reference "A reference is a local name with or without a prefix, as written in the script of a context."

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.writtenIn[FormalAmpersand.Reference*FormalAmpersand.Context] [UNI,TOT]
    MEANING "The context in whose script a reference is written."
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.prefix[FormalAmpersand.Reference*FormalAmpersand.Name] [UNI]
    MEANING "The prefix of a reference, if it has one."
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.localName[FormalAmpersand.Reference*FormalAmpersand.Name] [UNI,TOT]
    MEANING "The local name in a reference, which is its last part."

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.denotes[FormalAmpersand.Reference*FormalAmpersand.Context] [UNI,TOT]
    MEANING "The context in which a reference looks for its declaration."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.DenotesDefinition LABEL "definition of denotes" :
        FormalAmpersand.denotes
        = (I[FormalAmpersand.Reference] - FormalAmpersand.prefix[FormalAmpersand.Reference*FormalAmpersand.Name];FormalAmpersand.prefix[FormalAmpersand.Reference*FormalAmpersand.Name]~);FormalAmpersand.writtenIn
          \/ (FormalAmpersand.writtenIn;FormalAmpersand.includer~ /\ FormalAmpersand.prefix[FormalAmpersand.Reference*FormalAmpersand.Name];FormalAmpersand.prefix[FormalAmpersand.Inclusion*FormalAmpersand.Name]~);FormalAmpersand.included
    MEANING "A reference without a prefix denotes the context in which it is written. A reference with a prefix denotes the context that this context includes under that prefix."
    -- Since `denotes` is univalent, a prefix that fits two included contexts is an error: the script has to use an alias.
    -- Since `denotes` is total, a prefix that fits no inclusion is an error.

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.refersTo[FormalAmpersand.Reference*FormalAmpersand.Declaration]
    MEANING "A reference refers to a declaration if the script can mean that declaration by it."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.RefersToDefinition LABEL "definition of refersTo" :
        FormalAmpersand.refersTo = FormalAmpersand.denotes;FormalAmpersand.owner~
                                   /\ FormalAmpersand.localName[FormalAmpersand.Reference*FormalAmpersand.Name];FormalAmpersand.localName[FormalAmpersand.Declaration*FormalAmpersand.Name]~
    MEANING "A reference refers to the declarations with its local name that are owned by the context it denotes."
    -- Consequences (article, theorems on references):
    --   unambiguous: a reference refers to at most one concept, at most one rule,
    --                and at most one relation with a given source and target;
    --   sound:       refersTo |- writtenIn;visible~;
    --   complete:    every declaration of a context that A includes directly has a reference that is written in A.
    -- OPEN (naming): a prefix names a context that is included directly.
    --   A declaration of a context that A reaches in two steps is visible in A; to refer to it, A states the inclusion itself.
ENDPATTERN

PURPOSE PATTERN FormalAmpersand.ClassificationAcrossContexts
{+A classification `CLASSIFY S ISA G` says that every atom of the specific concept S is an atom of the generic concept G.
Within one context, S and G share one table.
Across contexts they cannot, because the population of a concept is stored in the database of its owner.
So the atoms of S are stored in the table of S, in the database of the owner of S,
and they are stored in the table of G as well, in the database of the owner of G.
The context that states the classification brings them there: it writes the owner of G.
That write is a change in the database of the owner of G like any other, so the owner of G guards its own rules against it.

A migration uses this to tell the type checker that a concept of the existing system and a concept of the desired system correspond:
`CLASSIFY old.A ISA new.A`, stated in the migration context.
+}
PATTERN FormalAmpersand.ClassificationAcrossContexts
    CONCEPT FormalAmpersand.Isa ""
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.gengen[FormalAmpersand.Isa*FormalAmpersand.Concept] [UNI,TOT]
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.genspc[FormalAmpersand.Isa*FormalAmpersand.Concept] [UNI,TOT]
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.context[FormalAmpersand.Isa*FormalAmpersand.Context] [UNI,TOT]

    RULE FormalAmpersand.ClassifiedConceptsAreNear LABEL "classified concepts are near" :
        (FormalAmpersand.gengen \/ FormalAmpersand.genspc)~;FormalAmpersand.context[FormalAmpersand.Isa*FormalAmpersand.Context]
        |- FormalAmpersand.context[FormalAmpersand.Concept*FormalAmpersand.Context];(I[FormalAmpersand.Context] \/ FormalAmpersand.includes~)
    MEANING "The generic and the specific concept of a classification belong to the context that states the classification or to a context that it includes directly."
    -- OPEN (classification): the owner of G can delete an atom from G that is still an atom of S.
    --   The classification is then violated, and the context that states it cannot refuse that change.
ENDPATTERN

PURPOSE PATTERN FormalAmpersand.ReadingAndWriting
{+An atom has no owner, and the same atom can occur in several databases.
What is stored has an owner.
That an atom is an instance of a concept is stored in the database of the owner of that concept, and nowhere else.
That a pair is in a relation is stored in the database of the owner of that relation, and nowhere else.
So every fact has one place where it is updated.

The backend of a context evaluates a term by reading every relation and every concept in that term from the database of its owner.
One term can therefore read several databases:
in `Shp.located;Reg.residence~` the pairs of `located` come from the database of the shops and the pairs of `residence` from the database of the register.
A context reads the databases of all contexts it reaches by inclusion.

A context writes its own database.
It writes the database of a context it includes directly in two cases:
one of its rules is enforced on a relation or a concept of that context,
or it states a classification whose generic concept belongs to that context.

A rule is guarded by the backend of its owner.
If a rule depends on a population that another context can write, that other context can violate the rule,
and the owner of the rule cannot refuse the change.
Such a rule is called exposed.
+}
PATTERN FormalAmpersand.ReadingAndWriting
    RELATION FormalAmpersand.reads[FormalAmpersand.Context*FormalAmpersand.Database]
    MEANING "A context reads a database if its backend evaluates terms on the population in that database."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.ReadsDefinition LABEL "definition of reads" :
        FormalAmpersand.reads = FormalAmpersand.includes*;FormalAmpersand.database
    MEANING "A context reads its own database and the databases of the contexts it reaches by inclusion."

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.enforces[FormalAmpersand.Rule*FormalAmpersand.Declaration]
    MEANING "A rule is enforced on a relation or a concept if the system restores the rule by changing the population of that relation or concept."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.EnforcedDeclarationIsNear LABEL "enforced declaration is near" :
        FormalAmpersand.enforces |- I[FormalAmpersand.Rule];FormalAmpersand.owner;(I[FormalAmpersand.Context] \/ FormalAmpersand.includes);FormalAmpersand.owner~
    MEANING "A rule is enforced on a declaration of its own context or of a context that its owner includes directly."

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.writes[FormalAmpersand.Context*FormalAmpersand.Context]
    MEANING "Context A writes context B if the backend of A changes the population in the database of B."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.WritesDefinition LABEL "definition of writes" :
        FormalAmpersand.writes = I[FormalAmpersand.Context]
                                 \/ FormalAmpersand.owner~;I[FormalAmpersand.Rule];FormalAmpersand.enforces;FormalAmpersand.owner
                                 \/ FormalAmpersand.context[FormalAmpersand.Isa*FormalAmpersand.Context]~;FormalAmpersand.gengen;FormalAmpersand.context[FormalAmpersand.Concept*FormalAmpersand.Context]
    MEANING "A context writes itself, the owner of every declaration on which one of its rules is enforced, and the owner of the generic concept of every classification it states."
    -- Consequence: writes |- I \/ includes, so a context writes only itself and contexts it includes directly.

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.exposedTo[FormalAmpersand.Rule*FormalAmpersand.Context]
    MEANING "A rule is exposed to a context if that context is not the owner of the rule and writes a population on which the rule depends."
    RULE FormalAmpersand.ExposedDefinition LABEL "definition of exposed" :
        FormalAmpersand.exposedTo = FormalAmpersand.dependsOn;FormalAmpersand.owner;FormalAmpersand.writes~ - I[FormalAmpersand.Rule];FormalAmpersand.owner
    MEANING "A rule is exposed to every context other than its owner that writes the owner of something the rule depends on."

    RELATION FormalAmpersand.isInvariant[FormalAmpersand.Rule] [PROP]
    MEANING "A rule is an invariant if the backend of its owner refuses every change that would violate it."
    -- OPEN (invariants): this is a signal for the designer and no hard requirement,
    --   because a migration lets an invariant of the migration context depend on the desired system, which is deployed and not yet in use.
    ROLE FormalAmpersand.Designer MAINTAINS FormalAmpersand.InvariantIsNotExposed
    RULE FormalAmpersand.InvariantIsNotExposed LABEL "invariant is not exposed" :
        FormalAmpersand.isInvariant;FormalAmpersand.exposedTo |- -V[FormalAmpersand.Rule*FormalAmpersand.Context]
    MEANING "An invariant is exposed to no context: its owner can refuse every change that would violate it."
ENDPATTERN

ENDCONTEXT
